body.proof_tex:
The construction of \(\widehat\tau_n\) contains neither \(\kappa\) nor an endpoint-class label.  We evaluate the oracle inequality in \(\mathrm{thm{:}profile\text{-}adaptive\text{-}estimator}\) at one deterministic common bandwidth for each endpoint class.

Fix \(\kappa\in K\), write \(\alpha=\kappa-2\), and first suppose that \(P\in\mathrm{Iso}_\kappa\).  Put
\[
 b_n=n^{-1/(\kappa+2)}.
\]
For all sufficiently large \(n\), uniformly over \(\kappa\in K\), the dyadic grid contains \(h_b\) such that
\[
 b_n\leq h_b<2b_n. \tag{E1}
\]
Indeed, eventually \(h_0>b_n\), while the smallest element of \(H_n\) is at most \(h_0/n<b_n\); two consecutive elements differ by a factor of two.  For \(x\in B\), define the proof intermediate
\[
 w_x=\frac{a_P(x,h_b)}{h_b^\kappa}.
\]
By \(\mathsf A_{\mathrm{mass}}\), \(w_x\geq c_m\).  Since increasing the oracle constant \(C_0\) only increases its right-hand side, take \(C_0\) large enough that \(C_0c_m\geq C_m\).  Then
\[
 u_x=\min\{1,C_0a_P(x,h_b)\}\geq C_mh_b^\kappa \tag{E2}
\]
for all sufficiently large \(n\), because \(C_mh_b^\kappa\leq1\) eventually.

If \(C_0w_xh_b^\kappa\leq1\), the isolated-envelope assumption at \(u_x=C_0w_xh_b^\kappa\) gives
\[
 \begin{aligned}
 J_P(h_b,u_x)
 &\leq C_{\mathrm{iso}}\left\{1+h_b^{-1}
 \left(\frac{C_0w_xh_b^\kappa}{h_b^2}\right)^{1/\alpha}\right\}\\
 &=C_{\mathrm{iso}}\left\{1+C_0^{1/\alpha}w_x^{1/\alpha}\right\}.
 \end{aligned} \tag{E3}
\]
If \(C_0w_xh_b^\kappa>1\), the same assumption at \(u_x=1\) gives
\[
 J_P(h_b,1)
 \leq C_{\mathrm{iso}}\left\{1+h_b^{-1-2/\alpha}\right\}. \tag{E4}
\]
The premise of this second case implies
\[
 w_x^{1/\alpha}>C_0^{-1/\alpha}h_b^{-\kappa/\alpha}
 =C_0^{-1/\alpha}h_b^{-1-2/\alpha}. \tag{E5}
\]
Thus, in both cases, compactness of \([\epsilon,\bar\kappa-2]\) yields a constant \(C\), uniform over \(\kappa\in K\), for which
\[
 1\vee J_P(h_b,u_x)\leq C\{1+w_x^{1/\alpha}\}. \tag{E6}
\]
Moreover,
\[
 \sup_{\alpha\in[\epsilon,\bar\kappa-2]}
 \sup_{w\geq c_m}
 \frac{1+\log\!\left(eC\{1+w^{1/\alpha}\}\right)}{w}<\infty. \tag{E7}
\]
To verify \((\mathrm{E7})\), compactness bounds \(1/\alpha\) above and below, so the numerator is at most \(C_K\{1+\log_+w\}\); the ratio \(\{1+\log_+w\}/w\) is bounded on \([c_m,\infty)\).

Using \(a_P(x,h_b)=w_xh_b^\kappa\), equations \((\mathrm{E6})\)--\((\mathrm{E7})\) imply
\[
 \begin{aligned}
 &Lh_b+\sigma\sqrt{
 \frac{1+\log\!\left(e\{1\vee J_P(h_b,u_x)\}\right)}
 {n a_P(x,h_b)}}\\
 &\qquad\leq Lh_b+\frac{C\sigma}{\sqrt{nh_b^\kappa}}
 \leq Cb_n. 
 \end{aligned} \tag{E8}
\]
The last inequality follows from \((\mathrm{E1})\), the upper bound \(\kappa\leq\bar\kappa\), and the identity \(nb_n^{\kappa+2}=1\).  It is uniform in \(x\), \(P\), and \(\kappa\in K\).  The oracle remainder obeys
\[
 \frac{\log^2(en)}{n}=o\!\left(n^{-1/(4+\epsilon)}\right)=o(b_n) \tag{E9}
\]
uniformly over \(K\), because \(1/(\kappa+2)\leq1/(4+\epsilon)\).  Evaluating the infimum in \(\mathrm{thm{:}profile\text{-}adaptive\text{-}estimator}\) at \(h_b\), taking the supremum over \(x\), and then over \(P\in\mathrm{Iso}_\kappa\), proves
\[
 \sup_{P\in\mathrm{Iso}_\kappa}
 E_P\|\widehat\tau_n-\tau_P\|_\infty
 \leq Cn^{-1/(\kappa+2)}. \tag{E10}
\]

For the pervasive endpoint, a bound valid on all of \(\mathrm{Base}_\kappa\) suffices.  If \(F\subseteq B\) is \(h\)-separated, order its second coordinates.  Consecutive coordinates differ by at least \(h\), while the segment has length one, and hence \(|F|\leq1+1/h\).  Since \(h\leq h_0\leq1/8\), the definition of \(J_P\) therefore gives, for every \(u\geq0\),
\[
 1\vee J_P(h,u)\leq\frac{2}{h}. \tag{E11}
\]
Put
\[
 r_n=\left(\frac{\log n}{n}\right)^{1/(\kappa+2)}
\]
and choose \(h_r\in H_n\) with \(r_n\leq h_r<2r_n\), which is possible for all sufficiently large \(n\), uniformly over \(K\), by the same dyadic-grid argument as for \((\mathrm{E1})\).  Assumption \(\mathsf A_{\mathrm{mass}}\), equation \((\mathrm{E11})\), and \(nr_n^{\kappa+2}=\log n\) yield
\[
 \begin{aligned}
 &\sup_{x\in B}\left[
 Lh_r+\sigma\sqrt{
 \frac{1+\log\!\left(e\{1\vee
 J_P(h_r,\min\{1,C_0a_P(x,h_r)\})\}\right)}
 {n a_P(x,h_r)}}\right]\\
 &\qquad\leq Lh_r+C\sigma
 \sqrt{\frac{\log(e/h_r)}{nc_mh_r^\kappa}}
 \leq Cr_n. 
 \end{aligned} \tag{E12}
\]
The constants in the final comparison are uniform because \(\kappa\in K\).  The remainder in the oracle theorem is \(o(r_n)\) uniformly on \(K\).  Since \(\mathrm{Perv}_\kappa\subseteq\mathrm{Base}_\kappa\), evaluating the theorem at \(h_r\) gives
\[
 \sup_{P\in\mathrm{Perv}_\kappa}
 E_P\|\widehat\tau_n-\tau_P\|_\infty
 \leq C\left(\frac{\log n}{n}\right)^{1/(\kappa+2)}. \tag{E13}
\]
Equations \((\mathrm{E10})\) and \((\mathrm{E13})\) use the identical total estimator, so no preliminary regime selection and no knowledge of \(\kappa\) is required.
